      <memberdef kind="enum" id="group__micro-os-plus-micro-test-plus-cpp-api-runners_1ga603af2d1c9643caa351305180b2e905b" prot="public" static="no" strong="yes">
        <type></type>
        <name>colour_mode</name>
        <qualifiedname>micro_os_plus::micro_test_plus::colour_mode</qualifiedname>
        <enumvalue id="group__micro-os-plus-micro-test-plus-cpp-api-runners_1gga603af2d1c9643caa351305180b2e905ba2bd9c0ed00116be1258e0cc66617d7c8" prot="public">
          <name>automatic</name>
          <briefdescription>
          </briefdescription>
          <detaileddescription>
<para>Colours are used when <computeroutput>stdout</computeroutput> is a terminal; on the platforms without <computeroutput>isatty()</computeroutput>, such as the embedded ones, they are not used. </para>
          </detaileddescription>
        </enumvalue>
        <enumvalue id="group__micro-os-plus-micro-test-plus-cpp-api-runners_1gga603af2d1c9643caa351305180b2e905baf9f90eeaf400d228facde6bc48da5cfb" prot="public">
          <name>always</name>
          <briefdescription>
          </briefdescription>
          <detaileddescription>
<para>Colours are always used, for example in the CI systems that render the ANSI escape sequences but do not provide a terminal, or on embedded targets whose output is displayed by a terminal. </para>
          </detaileddescription>
        </enumvalue>
        <enumvalue id="group__micro-os-plus-micro-test-plus-cpp-api-runners_1gga603af2d1c9643caa351305180b2e905bac7561db7a418dd39b2201dfe110ab4a4" prot="public">
          <name>never</name>
          <briefdescription>
          </briefdescription>
          <detaileddescription>
<para>Colours are never used. </para>
          </detaileddescription>
        </enumvalue>
        <briefdescription>
<para>The use of colours in the console output. </para>
        </briefdescription>
        <detaileddescription>
<para>Selected with the <computeroutput>--colour</computeroutput> command-line option (or its American spelling <computeroutput>--color</computeroutput>, or its alternative <computeroutput>--use-colour</computeroutput>, also spelled <computeroutput>--use-color</computeroutput>), or with <computeroutput><ref refid="classmicro__os__plus_1_1micro__test__plus_1_1runner__options_1a48ed4dd71eff02911c2d6943ba616850" kindref="member">runner_options::colour</ref></computeroutput>; the default is <computeroutput>automatic</computeroutput>. The names follow the values of <computeroutput>--color</computeroutput> in the GNU tools.</para>
<para>Only the human-readable reporter uses colours; the TAP reporter never does, since the ANSI escape sequences are not part of the TAP syntax. The output file never receives colours, whatever the mode.</para>
<para><simplesect kind="since"><para>6.0.0 </para>
</simplesect>
</para>
        </detaileddescription>
